Copyright (c) 2026 NCEA Studio. All rights reserved except as expressly granted below.

This is a custom source-available license. It is not an OSI-approved open-source
license. NCEA-CL-1.0 is a project shorthand, not an official SPDX identifier.

1. Definitions and scope

"Licensor" means NCEA Studio, only to the extent it owns or may license the
relevant copyright. "Licensed Material" means NCEA AntiCheat code, configuration,
documentation, and original materials in this repository for which Licensor
controls those rights. It excludes separately licensed third-party material and
material of uncertain provenance. "Fork" means a copy or derivative of Licensed
Material. "Distribution" means providing copies to others, including through a
source repository or JAR. Merely running a server is deployment, not Distribution.

"Functional Contribution" means an identifiable change that implements or
corrects behavior, security, compatibility, reliability, performance, or
maintainability. Examples include a real bug fix, new check, version adapter, or
architectural change with an explainable technical effect. A one-line fix can
qualify. No minimum percentage or line count applies. Changes limited to names,
logos, colors, package or class names without behavioral effect, README, plugin
messages, metadata, formatting, or other presentation do not qualify.

"Cosmetic Rebrand" means a Fork without a Functional Contribution that is
presented or distributed as a new independent product or with misleading
authorship. "You" means anyone exercising rights under this License.

2. Ownership

Licensor retains its copyright. Fork developers retain copyright in their own
original additions. This License assigns no ownership to either party.

3. Grant of permission

Subject to these terms, Licensor grants You a worldwide, non-exclusive,
royalty-free copyright permission to use, run, study, copy, fork, modify, test,
submit pull requests, publish changes, and distribute Licensed Material and
permitted Forks. The grant covers only rights Licensor controls. It grants no
patent or trademark rights.

4. Private work and public forks

You may experiment privately with any modification, including renaming. You may
maintain public branches and ordinary GitHub forks, including unchanged forks
that preserve the original identity and notices. You may publish Functional
Contributions and distribute Forks containing them under Section 8.

5. Cosmetic rebranding

You must not Distribute a Cosmetic Rebrand. Merely replacing a name, logo,
palette, author, package or class name, README, messages, or metadata does not
permit presenting a copy as independently developed. Cosmetic themes,
translations, or configurations may be shared if clearly identified as NCEA
AntiCheat variants and compliant with Sections 6 and 7. A renamed Fork with a
Functional Contribution is permitted if it meets all other terms.

6. Attribution and notices

Every Distribution must preserve existing copyright notices and include this
complete License. The source repository, its README or equivalent primary
documentation, and documentation accompanying a distributed binary must
prominently display these exact lines:

Originally developed by NCEA Studio
Original project: https://github.com/Yozekkk/NCEA-anticheat
Official website: https://ncea-studio.com

Each distributed binary must contain this License and these origin lines in a
LICENSE or NOTICE file. A Fork must also identify its developer, describe its
material Functional Contributions and other significant changes, and disclose
that it is unofficial unless separately authorized by Licensor. Additional
accurate copyright notices for new contributions are allowed.

7. Distribution of the original

You may redistribute an unchanged copy as NCEA AntiCheat, preserving its
identity, this License, all applicable third-party notices, and Section 6
attribution. You may identify Yourself as a distributor, but not as the
original developer.

8. Distribution of modified material

You may distribute a distinct Fork containing a Functional Contribution if
Sections 5, 6, 9, 10, and 11 are satisfied. Identify modified files or supply
an accessible change summary. Licensed Material and modifications to it must
remain available to recipients under this License; do not impose terms that
prevent exercise of these rights. Separable original additions may have other
terms if they do not negate this License for Licensed Material or violate a
third-party license. Source and binary distribution are allowed. This License
does not require publication of a Fork's complete source code.

9. Server deployment

Running the original or a Fork on a Minecraft server is permitted. The installed
plugin must retain an administrator-accessible origin notice, such as plugin
metadata, an about/version command, or an equivalent administrator interface.
It must identify NCEA Studio as original developer. A Fork must also identify
its own developer and give an accessible summary of material changes. Player
advertising is not required. Distribution of the JAR also triggers Sections 6-8.

10. Branding and marks

No rights in the names NCEA Studio or NCEA AntiCheat, their logos, or other
source-identifying marks are granted beyond truthful origin identification and
distribution of an unchanged copy under Section 7. Do not imply an unofficial
Fork is made, endorsed, or supported by NCEA Studio. Do not attribute a Fork's
changes to NCEA Studio or claim authorship of the original. "Based on NCEA
AntiCheat" is permitted when truthful and accompanied by Section 6 attribution.
This License does not claim any mark is registered. See TRADEMARKS.md.

11. Third-party material

This License does not relicense adapted AngelOS graphics, embedded fonts,
installed skills, dependencies, build tools, or other third-party works.
Their own terms govern. Preserve their notices. See docs/THIRD_PARTY_AUDIT.md
and docs/ASSET_CREDITS.md. A separate file-specific license takes precedence
for that file.

12. Termination and cure

Rights granted here terminate if You breach these conditions and fail to cure
within 30 days after receiving written notice describing the breach. Timely
cure reinstates the grant; otherwise written consent from Licensor is required.
Compliant recipients' existing rights and separate third-party licenses remain
unaffected.

